# Line endings.
#
# Git stores LF in the repository (core.autocrlf=true turns that into CRLF in a Windows working
# copy), and these rules decide which files a checkout gets back as LF. The ones a Linux shell,
# Docker or the database reads have to be LF even on Windows: a CRLF in a Dockerfile turns a "\"
# line continuation into a literal character and fails the build, a CRLF in a shell script makes the
# interpreter choke on the stray carriage return, and a CRLF in a .sql file can end up inside a
# string literal.
#
# The rest of this file used to be a copy of .gitignore, whose patterns set no attributes at all.
# Ignore rules belong in .gitignore, where they are.

/mvnw text eol=lf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

Dockerfile text eol=lf
.dockerignore text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.sql text eol=lf
*.properties text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
.mvn/** text eol=lf
